WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.940
Many films have been made about Elvis Presley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:07.732
but Baz Luhrmann's 2022 biopic is the first&nbsp;
to tackle the King's lonely final years.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.600
Baz Luhrmann's elaborate, often anachronistic&nbsp;
visual panache always gets a lot of attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.660
whether he's depicting 1890s Paris or 1920s New&nbsp;
York. Elvis is no exception, with its whirling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.700
roulette wheel camerawork and hip hop remixes of&nbsp;
Presley songs. But the film's most daring conceit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.840
is arguably the way it tells the story of Elvis&nbsp;
from the vantage point of Colonel Tom Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.680
played by Tom Hanks under a mountain of&nbsp;
prosthetics and a thick Dutch accent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.900
Parker looks back on his life with The King as&nbsp;
he lies dying in a Vegas hospital bed in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.140
wandering through a spectral casino&nbsp;
as the memories come flooding back.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.117
"I am the man who gave the world Elvis Presley."

00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:49.080
Over the years since Presley's death, Parker&nbsp;
has widely come to be seen as a villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.220
manipulating his one and only client's trust and&nbsp;
taking far more than his share of the profits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.120
Luhrmann's film doesn't disagree with this&nbsp;
version of the facts, but in Parker's mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.520
he is Elvis' other half, the carnival barker to&nbsp;
his sideshow freak, the snowman to his showman.

00:01:02.611 --> 00:01:07.335
"Col. Tom Parker was a carny, pure and simple."

00:01:07.372 --> 00:01:11.880
The film's most compelling tension is between&nbsp;
the blameless, almost victimized way Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.180
sees his own relationship with Elvis&nbsp;
compared with what we the audience see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.160
happening on screen. This tension is exposed&nbsp;
early in the film with a brief flash to 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.180
when Elvis collapses backstage before&nbsp;
a concert and Parker makes it perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.036
clear that the only thing that matters is&nbsp;
that he gets back on his feet and performs.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.480
As the film enters its final act, we revisit&nbsp;
that moment in 1974 when Presley collapses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.440
with added context. At this point, Elvis has&nbsp;
been performing at the International Hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.940
in Las Vegas for years due to the fact that&nbsp;
Parker signed him into essentially a lifetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.723
contract in exchange for the forgiveness&nbsp;
of Parker's massive gambling debts.

00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:48.423
"If I was you, I would book&nbsp;
him for a hundred years."

00:01:50.178 --> 00:01:52.539
"Well, no better time than the present."

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:55.320
Parker has also been promising&nbsp;
Presley his first global tour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.300
going back to the 1968 comeback special, only to&nbsp;
continually delay the start of the tour for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.440
reason or another. The truth finally comes to&nbsp;
light just moments before The King collapses:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.040
Parker keeps delaying the tour because&nbsp;
he came to the United States illegally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.871
and doesn't have a passport. His name isn't&nbsp;
even Tom Parker, nor was he ever a colonel.

00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:18.211
"But I hear rumors that Colonel is an alien."

00:02:18.211 --> 00:02:22.080
After his collapse, Presley is given a shot of&nbsp;
amphetamines to get him up and going again for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.220
the show. He takes the stage, inebriated&nbsp;
and incensed by his manager's betrayal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.000
and in spectacularly dramatic fashion, fires&nbsp;
Parker in the middle of his performance.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.240
"You are fired. You are fired. You're fired!"

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.360
It's a deeply satisfying moment, even if it is&nbsp;
nothing more than a fantasy. According to Elvis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.680
biographer Alanna Nash, Presley and Parker's&nbsp;
professional falling out occurred after a show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.060
not in the midst of one, and for a completely&nbsp;
different reason. Furthermore, Parker's immigrant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.900
status wouldn't come to light until after&nbsp;
Presley's death; there is no evidence that The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.980
King ever suspected that Parker was anyone other&nbsp;
than the West Virginia native he claimed to be.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.200
Parker's countermove, however, is accurate&nbsp;
to real life. After being fired by Presley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.360
Parker retires to his office and drafts an invoice&nbsp;
for unpaid promotional work dating back to 1955,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.980
totaling over $8 million. According to&nbsp;
Nash, this is based on a real incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.460
though the exact dollar amount&nbsp;
of the invoice is unknown. Elvis'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.760
father Vernon breaks the news to his son&nbsp;
that Parker's bill would bankrupt them.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.080
"We're gonna lose Graceland."

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.320
Elvis, furious, storms out of his suite,&nbsp;
only to find Parker waiting for him outside.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.820
The two men argue viciously. Elvis gnashes&nbsp;
teeth and threatens to kill Parker;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.960
the Colonel in turn belittles Presley's&nbsp;
career, reminding him, unfairly but honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.980
that the comeback special audience that loved&nbsp;
him so much was a plant, prompted to cheer by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.480
a flashing "applause" sign. The scene is in&nbsp;
many ways the climax of the film, and Butler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.320
and Hanks give it their all. Hanks delivers&nbsp;
the film's theme in this scene, declaring,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:57.660
"We are the same, you and I. We are two odd&nbsp;
lonely children, reaching for eternity."

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:01.899
His entreaty falls on deaf ears as Elvis&nbsp;
walks out of the Colonel's life forever.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.860
The film fades to black with a title card: "One&nbsp;
Year Later." Suddenly it's 1975 and Elvis is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.660
now a media curiosity, a real-life version of the&nbsp;
sideshow act Parker always considered him to be.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.560
"The colonel dressed up the hotel&nbsp;
like a carnival. There were posters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.062
teddy bears, and hound dogs all over the place."

00:04:19.062 --> 00:04:22.740
He's gained weight. He's separated from his&nbsp;
wife Priscilla and rarely sees his daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.280
Lisa Marie. And when he's not performing&nbsp;
in Las Vegas he's holed up like a hermit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.780
at Graceland. There is one glimmer of hope,&nbsp;
as talks are apparently ongoing to have him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.400
star with Barbra Streisand in a remake of A Star&nbsp;
is Born, but we know it will never come to be.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.480
On the tarmac alongside the private jet&nbsp;
he named after his daughter, Elvis and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.780
Priscilla have a tender conversation.&nbsp;
Like most of the dialogue in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.100
its primary focus is to establish for us the&nbsp;
state of Elvis' life and career in this moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.900
and what his dreams and goals are. Elvis still&nbsp;
wants to make one good movie, after dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.886
cheap musicals, and worries that nothing he's&nbsp;
done will amount to anything when he dies.

00:04:56.356 --> 00:05:02.880
"Nobody's gonna remember me. I've&nbsp;
never done anything lasting."

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.600
The film presents this as Elvis' final&nbsp;
conversation with his ex-wife, although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.115
in reality, he would live for another two years.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.560
From that scene on the tarmac, the film skips&nbsp;
ahead two years to the announcement that Presley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.480
has been found dead in Graceland at age 42.&nbsp;
Rather than try to recreate the spectacle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.140
Elvis' funeral procession or the massive&nbsp;
outpouring of public grief that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:26.640
Luhrmann uses archival footage of the real&nbsp;
thing. He also includes an audio excerpt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.820
from President Jimmy Carter's statement&nbsp;
on Presley's death, in which he said,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.740
Not too shabby for a kid who scandalized&nbsp;
the nation just two decades earlier.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.640
In choosing not to recreate Elvis'&nbsp;
funeral, the film also skips over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.720
fact that Parker reportedly wore a Hawaiian&nbsp;
shirt and baseball cap to the service and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.380
while there, tried to strike deals with&nbsp;
mourners, according to biographer James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:57.120
Dickerson. We'll never know whether his behavior&nbsp;
came out of callousness, sociopathy, denial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.900
or some misguided sense of showmanship,&nbsp;
just to keep everyone talking about him.

00:06:09.147 --> 00:06:13.227
Butler up as the so-called "fat Elvis," but&nbsp;
here the young actor is decked out in wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.227 --> 00:06:17.187
pair of jowls and double chin, mumbling and&nbsp;
stumbling as he sits down at the piano and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.187 --> 00:06:20.547
belts out an emotional rendition of the&nbsp;
Righteous Brothers' "Unchained Melody."

00:06:20.547 --> 00:06:24.447
Sweat pouring down his face, the camera&nbsp;
stays tight on Butler in an intimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.447 --> 00:06:28.827
slightly uncomfortable way. It's different from&nbsp;
the way any of the film's previous musical numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.827 --> 00:06:33.627
have been shot, but soon we understand the reason&nbsp;
why, as Luhrmann cuts from Butler as Elvis to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.627 --> 00:06:38.007
footage of The King himself singing, accompanied&nbsp;
by a short montage of the real-life Elvis in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.007 --> 00:06:42.387
of the situations we've seen dramatized in the&nbsp;
film. It's an emotionally affecting transition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.387 --> 00:06:47.307
as we see just how accurately Luhrmann and Butler&nbsp;
have recreated so many moments in Presley's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.307 --> 00:06:52.587
It's also a reminder of just how powerful Elvis'&nbsp;
voice and presence were, even in his final days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.587 --> 00:06:58.167
He gives a showstopper performance at that piano,&nbsp;
and the audience hangs on every note. We still do.

00:06:59.007 --> 00:07:03.867
In the end, Elvis can't speak for himself, so&nbsp;
Parker does it for him, as he did for most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.867 --> 00:07:09.027
their life together. Parker ponders who or what&nbsp;
killed Elvis as he shuffles about his purgatorial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.027 --> 00:07:13.767
casino floor in a hospital gown, dragging&nbsp;
an IV pole. It wasn't the pills and drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:18.087
administered by doctors who should have known&nbsp;
better, enabled by Parker, Vernon, and Presley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.087 --> 00:07:22.647
whole entourage, the so-called "Memphis Mafia,"&nbsp;
who were all complicit in working Elvis to death.

00:07:22.994 --> 00:07:25.094
"What can you do for him, Dr. Nick?"

00:07:28.527 --> 00:07:32.067
And it wasn't Parker's greed and&nbsp;
mismanagement of Elvis' business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.067 --> 00:07:35.667
which forced him to continue performing&nbsp;
at an unforgiving pace for years;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:39.087
Parker is all too quick to absolve&nbsp;
himself of that responsibility.

00:07:39.087 --> 00:07:40.867
"I didn't kill him.

00:07:42.035 --> 00:07:45.785
I made Elvis Presley." 

00:07:45.785 --> 00:07:50.487
No, of course, it's we who killed Elvis, with&nbsp;
our relentless love for him and his inability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.487 --> 00:07:55.767
say no. Parker's monologue here is a fascinating&nbsp;
moment in the film; Luhrmann and Hanks keep Col.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.767 --> 00:08:00.147
Parker at arm's length, unwilling to tell the&nbsp;
audience how we should feel about him. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:04.167
they let the dying Dutch carny, the&nbsp;
"snowman," snow us to the very end.

00:08:05.007 --> 00:08:09.567
After the screen fades to black, we get a series&nbsp;
of post-scripts filling us in on how the saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.567 --> 00:08:13.947
of Elvis and the Colonel played out in the years&nbsp;
after Presley's death. The film takes liberties in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.947 --> 00:08:17.907
regard to what Presley and the rest of the world&nbsp;
knew about Parker's background. In real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.907 --> 00:08:22.467
it would be 1980 before it would be confirmed&nbsp;
that Parker was of Dutch descent and entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:27.327
the country illegally as a 30-year-old man.&nbsp;
Eventually, though, Parker's financial malfeasance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.327 --> 00:08:32.607
would come to light, and, in 1982, after a series&nbsp;
of lawsuits filed by Elvis Presley Enterprises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.607 --> 00:08:36.567
a judge ruled that Parker had no legal&nbsp;
claim to any part of the Presley estate.

00:08:36.567 --> 00:08:41.907
In his final two decades, Parker spent his days&nbsp;
in Las Vegas, puttering around one casino floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.907 --> 00:08:46.767
or another, pouring his immense wealth down&nbsp;
the drain. He made few public appearances or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.767 --> 00:08:51.387
statements, emerging from the shadows only on&nbsp;
rare occasions such as the 1993 release of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.387 --> 00:08:56.007
Elvis postage stamp. Presley, meanwhile, far from&nbsp;
the forgotten lounge singer he feared he'd become,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.007 --> 00:09:00.747
remains an international superstar nearly 50 years&nbsp;
after his death, and one of the highest-earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.747 --> 00:09:05.787
dead celebrities of all time. His life and&nbsp;
work still inspire admiration and controversy.

